 "highlights": [
  "18 of 18 respondents (100%) have known a photo was in Google Photos and still failed to find it; 15 (83%) say this happens “Sometimes” or “Frequently”.",
  "17 of 18 (94%) find a failed search at least slightly frustrating; 4 (22%) call it very or extremely frustrating. Mean frustration: 2.72 / 5.",
  "Top reason for failure: “I don't remember enough details” (5 of 18). Second: “I don't know what words to search” (4).",
  "The clues people remember first are person/persons (13), event/occasion (10), approximate time/period (7).",
  "9 of 18 (50%) would prefer Google Photos to ask clarifying questions when it doesn’t understand them — the most-picked interaction.",
  "After a failed search, the most common next step is “add more details” (7); 4 fall back to browsing the timeline.",
  "Only 12 of 18 (67%) are confident they can describe a half-remembered item well enough for search to find it.",
  "Most-wanted narrowing filters: place (9), approximate date/time (8), person (8).",
  "Less confident respondents are more frustrated: mean frustration 3.67 for “somewhat unsure” (n=3) vs 1.5 for “very confident” (n=2) — directional, small groups.",
  "Frustration climbs with failure frequency: 2 for “rarely” (n=3) vs 3.2 for “frequently” (n=5)."
 ],
